Feature: ReportPortal
  ReportPortal keeps test runs as launches with their history and shows them in the browser. It runs in a namespace of its own that a cluster's overlay selects, from its publisher's chart on its classic Helm repository, with its database from the PostgreSQL component and its files on a backed-up volume, reached at its own hostname on the public Gateway behind the cluster's sign-in and then its own login. Its superadmin's first password and RabbitMQ's credentials are generated in the cluster, so nothing secret is in the tree and nothing is seeded in the store, and its API Service takes launches from inside the cluster once a rule names their pods.

  Scenario: ReportPortal is selected by overlay entries and builds nothing when it is not
    Given the lab-sto2 overlay, which selects ReportPortal with four entries, the namespace directory reportportal and the ks.yaml of reportportal, reportportal-route and reportportal-volsync-movers
    When it is built with the four entries, with only the namespace directory's and with none, and then with each application they depend on left out in turn
    Then with the four it applies the namespace reportportal, its app-template-defaults ConfigMap, its default-deny NetworkPolicy and the three Kustomizations: reportportal, which takes the PostgreSQL and volume components for reportportal-db and the claim reportportal-shared-volume-claim, the long timeout, waits and names cloudnative-pg, plugin-barman-cloud, prometheus-operator-crds, external-secrets, openbao-store, volsync, linstor-cluster and cilium; reportportal-route, which takes the sign-in component, waits and names reportportal, gateway-public, oauth2-proxy and blackbox-exporter; and reportportal-volsync-movers, which waits and names cilium; with only the namespace directory's, the namespace, the ConfigMap and the NetworkPolicy and nothing of ReportPortal; with none, nothing of the namespace; with one of the dependencies left out it is refused, naming the Kustomization and the missing application

  Scenario: ReportPortal's chart and every image it runs are pinned by version and digest and move together
    Given the HelmRelease, its HelmRepository and its values as the lab-sto2 overlay applies them, the chart rendered with the release's post-renderers, and the Renovate rules and records
    When the chart's source and version, every image of every pod the chart renders, the image tags of the values, the Renovate comments, the extraction record and the signature row are read
    Then the chart is reportportal 26.11.18 from ReportPortal's classic Helm repository, whose row of signatures.yaml is unsigned; the four ReportPortal images, the ui's copy of its own image, k8s-wait-for, kubectl and RabbitMQ are docker.io references by tag and sha256 digest, with IfNotPresent; the four ReportPortal tags equal each other and the version the chart's own values name; each pin is under its Renovate comment and extracted by Renovate exactly once; one Renovate group moves the chart and the four images together, and nothing of the application merges itself

  Scenario: ReportPortal runs the ui, the API, the jobs service and RabbitMQ, and nothing of the analyzer, the search engine, the bundled stores or the paid tier
    Given the chart rendered with the release's values and post-renderers
    When its workloads, Services, claims, Ingresses, routes and hooks and the release's timeout are read
    Then it runs the Deployments reportportal-ui, reportportal-api and reportportal-jobs with one replica each, the StatefulSet reportportal-rabbitmq, the Job reportportal-migrations and the pre-upgrade hook that deletes it, whose own Job Helm deletes before making it again as well as once it succeeds; an upgrade has the install's ten minutes; no analyzer, OpenSearch, SeaweedFS, MinIO, PostgreSQL, MCP server or paid component, no chart claim, PodDisruptionBudget, Ingress, Gateway or HTTPRoute, no test hook and no image of the paid tier; the api and the jobs service mount the component's claim at /data/storage, keep their files there by the filesystem storage type and run with the Recreate strategy, the jobs pod required on the api's node and the api preferring the jobs pod's; and each container asks for the chart's requests, RabbitMQ for 100m and 256Mi with a 512Mi limit and a 1Gi volume on the default class

  Scenario: Every pod of ReportPortal runs at the restricted level as a non-root user with a read-only root
    Given the namespace reportportal as the overlay builds it, the exceptions list and every pod of the render, the hook included
    When the namespace's labels are read, each pod's and container's security context and volumes are read, and each pod is evaluated at the Pod Security levels
    Then the namespace enforces restricted and no exception names it; every pod passes restricted; the api and jobs run as user and group 1000, the ui as 101, the migrations as 65532 and the hook as 1000, each non-root with the runtime's seccomp profile; every container and init container of the api, the ui, the jobs service, the migrations and the hook drops every capability, forbids privilege escalation and has a read-only root; the api writes /tmp and /var/log/reportportal, the jobs service /tmp, and the ui /tmp, /var/cache/nginx and its html directory, each an emptyDir, the html directory filled by an init container of the ui's own image; and the ui and migrations pods mount no service account token

  Scenario: ReportPortal's namespace denies traffic by default and lets through only the flows its policies declare
    Given the namespace's default deny, the policies of the application and of its movers, the proxies' policy and the render
    When each policy's rules and the api's and the jobs service's environment are read and the flows between ReportPortal's pods, the database, the proxies, Prometheus, the API server, the kubelet, another namespace and the world are evaluated
    Then the namespace holds the default deny and is not listed; the ui takes the proxies and the kubelet on 8080 and sends nothing; the api takes the proxies and the kubelet on 8585 and reaches the resolver, the database's instances on 5432, RabbitMQ on 5672 and 15672, the jobs service on 8686 and the API server; the jobs service takes the api and the kubelet on 8686 and reaches the resolver, the database, RabbitMQ and the API server; the migrations reach the resolver and the database; the hook reaches the API server alone; RabbitMQ takes the api and the jobs service on 5672 and 15672 and sends nothing; the database's pods and the dump job let through what Backstage's do; the proxies reach the ui on 8080 and the api on 8585 and nothing else of the namespace; and the proxies to the jobs service, RabbitMQ or the database, another namespace, robot included, to the api, the ui to anything, and the api and the jobs service to the world are denied; and what the two would call outside on their own is off in their environment, the api's plugin fetch at start by RP_PLUGINS_DEFAULT_LOAD false and the jobs service's usage statistics to Google by RP_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE_GA_MID and RP_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE_GA_ID empty

  Scenario: ReportPortal answers at its own hostname only behind the cluster's sign-in, by the chart's own paths, and its probe expects the redirect to sign-in
    Given the application reportportal-route built as Flux applies it on lab-sto2, with the example key lists
    When the namespace's labels, the HTTPRoute, the sign-in component's objects, oauth2-proxy's grant, every route and ReferenceGrant of the namespace, the tree's other routes, the Services and pods of the release and of its applications and the Probe are read, the route is compared with the chart's own at the pinned version, and the application is built again for another cluster domain
    Then the namespace carries the Gateway label; the route reportportal on the https listener of public sends reportportal under the cluster's domain with the prefix /api to reportportal-api on 8585 and everything else to reportportal-ui on 8080, with no filter, as the chart's own route does at the version its record names; the SecurityPolicy of the sign-in component covers it and its sign-in route sends /oauth2/ to oauth2-proxy; oauth2-proxy's grant names reportportal for both; the namespace holds no route but these two and no ReferenceGrant, and no other route of the tree names ReportPortal; no Service of it is a LoadBalancer or a NodePort and no pod of it takes the node's network or a port of it; no firewall is in front; the Probe is hello-sso's at / with the module that expects the redirect to sign-in; and built for another domain, the route's hostname, the policy's return address and the Probe's all name it

  Scenario: ReportPortal's database comes from the PostgreSQL component, its files are on a backed-up volume, and no secret is in the tree
    Given the application reportportal built as Flux applies it on lab-sto2, with the example key lists, and the render
    When the Cluster, the dump job, the volume's claim and its backup, the Password generators, the ExternalSecrets, the release's database, broker and superadmin settings and every object built and rendered are read
    Then the Cluster reportportal-db has the component's three instances and 5Gi each, and its dump job's pods carry the labels its policies select; the api, the jobs service and the migrations read the database's address, user and name from the values and its password from the operator's Secret reportportal-db-app, the migrations over TLS; the claim reportportal-shared-volume-claim is the volume component's, 5Gi on the default class, backed up daily at 03:53, a minute no other backup of the cluster starts at, and kept daily, weekly and monthly as every volume is; External Secrets generates the superadmin's password and RabbitMQ's password and Erlang cookie once each, in hex, the superadmin's short enough for the login form, into the Secrets the release names; and nothing built or rendered is a Secret, holds a literal password, secret key or access key, or is the chart's own password Secret or RabbitMQ's
